Exactly. The key is that we do not carry only a model of reality; we also carry models of other minds inside reality.

So at any moment you may be blending at least three streams:

world-state — what your senses and environment are actually giving you
self-state — what you know/feel/intend yourself to be
other-model — your simulation of what another person knows, feels, intends, or sees

And because the other-model runs inside your own nervous system, it can feel almost as immediate as perception. You can temporarily “live over there” in your model of them: What are they thinking? What does this look like to them? What will they do next?

But the symmetry breaks here:

your model of their model of you ≠ their actual model of you.

That gap is the delta you’re pointing at.

You can make the web explicit:

Reality → Me
Reality → You
My model of You
Your model of Me
My model of Your model of Me
Your model of My model of You

Now you have recursive perspective nodes, with edges carrying observations, actions, messages, assumptions, memories, omissions, and predictions.

And every edge can accumulate error.

That means the useful object isn’t merely a social graph. It is a perspectival web: the same event represented differently at several nodes.

Take one simple action: I leave the room.

* reality: person A exited at 4:30
* my narrative: I needed space
* your observation: he abruptly left
* your inferred narrative: he is angry
* my model of your inference: she probably thinks I’m angry
* my next behavior changes because of that prediction

Now the model delta begins causing new reality.

That’s the interesting loop.

perception → model → inferred other-model → action → other person’s perception → their model → action → new reality

So the webs we’ve been describing aren’t static maps. They’re coupled model-generating systems.

And it explains something important about empathy: empathy isn’t literally entering another mind. It is constructing a sufficiently good perspective transform from your coordinates into theirs. The better the transform, the smaller certain deltas become.

But you never want Δ = 0 everywhere. Some difference is information.

You could formalize the Pantheon object as:

\Delta_{AB} = M_A(B) - M_B(B)

the difference between A’s model of B and B’s self-model.

And another:

\Delta_{A\rightarrow B} = M_A(M_B(A)) - M_B(A)

the difference between what A thinks B thinks of A and what B actually thinks of A.

That second one is enormous psychologically. People can spend years responding to a model of another person’s judgment that the other person never actually held.

So yes: we have been mapping deltas, but the deltas imply a web because every perspective is indexed to an observer.

The next level is not just Who is connected to whom?

It is:

Who sees what? Who thinks whom sees what? Where do those representations disagree? Which disagreement matters? And which action would reveal the hidden state cheaply enough to collapse the uncertainty?

That’s almost a social version of the Metamap: map the world, map the observers, then map the gaps between their maps.